; The same three-segment map, conducted by an IT anchor through the
; timing walk (plan 03, rule 3).
;
; Speed 6 and 24 ticks to the quarter make a row a 16th note, so row 4 is
; 1q and row 8 is 2q. `T96` sets the tempo to 150 and `TC8` to 200.
; Pattern 1 is the one-row `B01` that jumps to itself: the walk ends at
; the second visit of that row, with the state it left pattern 0 in — so
; the module plays its tempo changes ONCE and the segment table is the
; three the native twin authored.
;
; The highlight `[4 16]` is 4 rows to the beat and 16 to the measure, so
; the meter is one point at 0 with [16/4 4] = [4 4] — the same meter the
; other two spell by hand.
;
; `song.it` beside it is the file this dump came from, generated by `zig
; build fixtures` and verified against the anchor's `:size` and `:sha256`
; before the dump's offsets are honoured — the rule
; `tempo-three-conductors-smf` states. It is written `:loose` (16- and
; 1-row patterns, under IT 2.14's floor of 32) so the row arithmetic
; above can be read rather than padded, which is also why
; `(build :kind it)` — which writes STRICT — produces a longer file and
; says so in two diagnostics.
(timeline :length 1bar :conductor song)

(anchor :id song :kind it :file "song.it" :size 247 :sha256 "410f3cbde730eaf725d6ee812a759dc3dc5d8d37471e75de6c914cabd7cfa12f")

(it/module
  (it/timing :speed 6 :tempo 125 :highlight [4 16])
  (it/options :instruments false)
  (it/orders 0 1)
  (it/pattern 0 :rows 16
    (row 4 (ev 1 :fx "T96"))
    (row 8 (ev 1 :fx "TC8")))
  (it/pattern 1 :rows 1
    (row 0 (ev 1 :fx "B01"))))
